1
00:00:00,040 --> 00:00:02,000
Have you ever encountered this situation?

2
00:00:02,000 --> 00:00:04,120
Your resume is written really impressively.

3
00:00:04,120 --> 00:00:05,600
But then the interviewer asks a question,

4
00:00:05,600 --> 00:00:06,380
and you're completely stumped.

5
00:00:06,380 --> 00:00:08,960
Or have you ever come across a resume like this?

6
00:00:08,960 --> 00:00:09,400
Hmm,

7
00:00:09,400 --> 00:00:10,580
you think the resume is really impressive,

8
00:00:10,580 --> 00:00:11,980
but when the person shows up,

9
00:00:11,980 --> 00:00:12,980
they can't deliver.

10
00:00:12,980 --> 00:00:16,500
So, how do we identify these overly exaggerated resumes?

11
00:00:16,500 --> 00:00:18,780
The first type is the overuse of adjectives.

12
00:00:18,780 --> 00:00:20,600
This is a typical tactic of exaggerated resumes.

13
00:00:20,600 --> 00:00:23,750
For example, the About section states excellent communication and coordination skills.

14
00:00:23,750 --> 00:00:25,650
'extensive project management experience',

15
00:00:25,650 --> 00:00:27,310
or 'significantly improved efficiency'.

16
00:00:27,310 --> 00:00:29,750
But tell me, how exactly are they excellent?

17
00:00:29,750 --> 00:00:30,470
How extensive is it?

18
00:00:30,470 --> 00:00:31,210
And how significant is the improvement?

19
00:00:31,210 --> 00:00:33,290
So, how should we write it correctly?

20
00:00:33,290 --> 00:00:34,650
We start with a verb.

21
00:00:34,650 --> 00:00:37,250
For example, leading the setup and optimization.

22
00:00:37,250 --> 00:00:38,270
Then we add context.

23
00:00:38,270 --> 00:00:39,590
And include data support.

24
00:00:39,590 --> 00:00:43,350
For instance, we led the optimization of a CRM system in the financial sector.

25
00:00:43,350 --> 00:00:46,990
We achieved this through the analysis of business behaviors and the restructuring of processes.

26
00:00:46,990 --> 00:00:48,570
We focus on the conversion rate of customer service

27
00:00:48,570 --> 00:00:50,510
from 3% to 10%.

28
00:00:50,510 --> 00:00:51,550
Just one sentence

29
00:00:51,550 --> 00:00:52,670
Explain your actions

30
00:00:52,670 --> 00:00:53,390
Demonstrate your skills

31
00:00:53,390 --> 00:00:55,220
And clearly state your outcomes

32
00:00:55,220 --> 00:00:56,100
R1, look

33
00:00:56,100 --> 00:00:56,940
This person is really impressive

34
00:00:56,940 --> 00:00:57,860
He’s not exaggerating

35
00:00:57,860 --> 00:01:00,940
The second type of empty talk is a list of routine tasks

36
00:01:00,940 --> 00:01:02,840
This resume lacks any focus

37
00:01:02,840 --> 00:01:04,260
Many students write their resumes

38
00:01:04,260 --> 00:01:07,800
listing their activities one by one in a chronological manner

39
00:01:07,800 --> 00:01:09,660
including market research

40
00:01:09,660 --> 00:01:10,880
data analysis and prototyping

41
00:01:10,880 --> 00:01:12,150
design requirements

42
00:01:12,150 --> 00:01:13,110
product writing

43
00:01:13,110 --> 00:01:14,810
covering the entire lifecycle of a project

44
00:01:14,810 --> 00:01:16,350
which seems like a lot of work

45
00:01:16,350 --> 00:01:17,630
but tell me

46
00:01:17,630 --> 00:01:18,710
In this role,

47
00:01:18,710 --> 00:01:20,480
what specific responsibilities did you have?

48
00:01:20,480 --> 00:01:21,920
What exactly did you do?

49
00:01:21,920 --> 00:01:23,240
What value did you bring?

50
00:01:23,240 --> 00:01:24,320
It’s not reflected at all.

51
00:01:24,320 --> 00:01:25,960
So, what’s the correct logic here?

52
00:01:25,960 --> 00:01:28,289
You need to clearly highlight your role.

53
00:01:28,289 --> 00:01:29,809
As the product lead,

54
00:01:29,809 --> 00:01:33,320
and what was the goal of the project you managed?

55
00:01:33,320 --> 00:01:37,200
We are responsible for the GMV and the user experience.

56
00:01:37,200 --> 00:01:38,870
What kind of outcome do we aim to achieve?

57
00:01:38,870 --> 00:01:41,870
Then we break down our actions.

58
00:01:41,870 --> 00:01:44,620
For instance, as the product lead of an AI recommendation system

59
00:01:44,620 --> 00:01:47,789
we take the lead in building it from scratch.

60
00:01:47,789 --> 00:01:51,409
Through algorithm iterations and profile upgrades,

61
00:01:51,409 --> 00:01:55,530
We ultimately increased the click rate by how much

62
00:01:55,530 --> 00:01:57,699
And we also aim to boost the user retention rate by a certain percentage.

63
00:01:57,699 --> 00:02:01,779
In a nutshell, we define our role, set our goals, and take action.

64
00:02:01,779 --> 00:02:03,630
Everything has been explained clearly.

65
00:02:03,630 --> 00:02:05,710
Then it's evident that this person is reliable.

66
00:02:05,710 --> 00:02:06,990
He's not just going through the motions.

67
00:02:06,990 --> 00:02:09,669
The third scenario is also a common mistake made by many students.

68
00:02:09,669 --> 00:02:12,170
It means listing out all the modules you've worked on.

69
00:02:12,170 --> 00:02:14,180
Like 1, 2, 3, 4, 5, 6.

70
00:02:14,180 --> 00:02:15,340
You've done a lot of work.

71
00:02:15,340 --> 00:02:17,060
But what problems have actually been solved?

72
00:02:17,060 --> 00:02:18,100
It's not at all clear.

73
00:02:18,100 --> 00:02:21,000
For example, building modules for event configurations

74
00:02:21,000 --> 00:02:22,700
the recommended configuration modules

75
00:02:22,700 --> 00:02:24,040
Data monitoring reports

76
00:02:24,040 --> 00:02:25,800
Or any other functional modules

77
00:02:25,800 --> 00:02:28,269
So, if you tell me you've built all these modules

78
00:02:28,269 --> 00:02:29,749
What skills do they demonstrate?

79
00:02:29,749 --> 00:02:31,509
What pain points do they address?

80
00:02:31,509 --> 00:02:32,809
What value do they bring?

81
00:02:32,809 --> 00:02:34,120
They still don't demonstrate anything

82
00:02:34,120 --> 00:02:35,960
So, what is the right approach?

83
00:02:35,960 --> 00:02:39,940
We should always link our actions to the goal.

84
00:02:39,940 --> 00:02:42,420
It's not about having more modules, but about their effectiveness.

85
00:02:42,420 --> 00:02:43,560
Make it clear.

86
00:02:43,560 --> 00:02:46,240
From collaborative implementation to efficiency improvement,

87
00:02:46,240 --> 00:02:47,720
and then to cost optimization, the logic chain should be clear.

88
00:02:47,720 --> 00:02:49,060
It should provide a comprehensive demonstration.

89
00:02:49,060 --> 00:02:53,030
For example, we built a content operation platform from scratch.

90
00:02:53,030 --> 00:02:54,950
Breaking it down into resource management,

91
00:02:54,950 --> 00:02:55,930
Content management

92
00:02:55,930 --> 00:02:57,810
data monitoring, and these three capabilities

93
00:02:57,810 --> 00:02:59,010
So we have achieved

94
00:02:59,010 --> 00:03:02,110
an operational staff usage rate of 90%

95
00:03:02,110 --> 00:03:05,070
and the cost has been reduced by 300 yuan each month

96
00:03:05,070 --> 00:03:07,110
summarize your technical skills

97
00:03:07,110 --> 00:03:08,200
your understanding of the business

98
00:03:08,200 --> 00:03:10,160
and all the value you've produced, in one sentence

99
00:03:10,160 --> 00:03:14,440
This shows HR that this person is not just a coding role

100
00:03:14,440 --> 00:03:16,290
not just tech-savvy

101
00:03:16,290 --> 00:03:17,530
but also business-savvy

102
00:03:17,530 --> 00:03:19,810
Actually, let's summarize the writing style of an excellent resume

103
00:03:19,810 --> 00:03:22,280
which is the implicit application of the four major rules

104
00:03:22,280 --> 00:03:23,480
So what are these four major rules?

105
00:03:23,480 --> 00:03:24,420
They are situational

106
00:03:24,420 --> 00:03:25,240
task-oriented

107
00:03:25,240 --> 00:03:25,960
action-driven

108
00:03:25,960 --> 00:03:26,620
and result-focused

109
00:03:26,620 --> 00:03:27,980
write around this logic

